selection after a knockout.\n\n**Behavior delta:** When ranking own in-play PokÃ©mon for promotion, an already-attack-ready rule-box attacker (Mega Venusaur ex â†’ `megaEx`; Hydrapple ex / Teal Mask Ogerpon ex â†’ `ex`) now receives a bonus of `120 * prize_value + usable_damage * 5`. The `120 * prize_value` term exactly cancels the prize-liability penalty inside `_live_attacker_score` (which otherwise nudges promotion toward a cheap support chump), and the `usable_damage * 5` term adds a mild preference for the attacker that can deal the most usable damage *right now*.\n\n**Why it stays narrow (preserving generic scoring elsewhere):**\n- The bonus is gated on `usable_damage > 0`. At setup no PokÃ©mon is powered, so it is silent there â€” it only fires when a rule-box attacker is already attack-ready, i.e. exactly the mid-game post-knockout promotion the hypothesis targets.\n- It only affects the own-board CARD ranking. Opponent-targeting, discard/return, evolve, attach, MAIN-phase greedy/lookahead, `_eval_state`, and all ability handling are unchanged.\n- No global evaluation change: `_eval_state` and the search rollout are untouched, so the lookahead's board scoring is identical.\n\n## Concerns for the human reviewer\n\n1. **Path assumption:** This assumes forced promotion after a KO is delivered to the agent as a `CARD`-family select whose options are the player's own bench PokÃ©mon (so it enters the `own_board_indices` branch). This matches the existing \"ponytail\" note at line ~292 asserting attacker choice is decided at forced promotion, but it should be confirmed against a live decision trace that the after-KO promotion actually routes through `_choose_card` rather than a distinct select type that bypasses it.\n2. **Magnitude tuning is unmeasured:** The `120 * prize_value` cancellation is exact by construction, but the `dmg * 5` weight is a judgment call. Analysis suggests the existing `dmg * 20` term already dominates most promotions, so the net effect may be small â€” the benchmark should confirm the change moves the intended metric (attacks by primary attackers / turns lost after KO) rather than being within noise, and an ablation of the two terms would isolate which part carries any gain.\n3. **Cross-deck neutrality:** Because the trigger requires an attack-ready `ex`/`megaEx`, decks without powered rule-box attackers in that slot should see no change â€” worth a spot-check against the configured opponents to confirm no regression.","ttft_ms":1608,"ttft_stream_ms":1528,"time_to_request_ms":184,"type":"result","duration_ms":141129,"uuid":"0e6cd5c2-ee5b-4be3-87ef-419e6334c248"}
